Title: RUTH
Credit: Sample scene for review
Source: Ruth 1:6–18 · Treatment v3, Act One: "Leaving" and "The edge"
Draft date: 2 October 2026
Notes: Series working title: Adonai

/*
HOW TO READ THIS

This is plain-text screenplay format (Fountain). Open it in Highland, Beat or Slugline to see it laid out as a screenplay, or just read it as it is.

Anything in [[double brackets]] is a working note. Notes don't appear in a rendered script. They say where each line or moment comes from:

  A, B, C, D   dialogue tiers: locked, recorded, implied, invented (ROADMAP §4.2)
  P1–P6        content levels: Scripture, history, tradition, inference, your conviction, invention (ROADMAP §4.1)
  ·n           the item's number in the treatment's additions register (TREATMENT §3)

Accents: RUTH, ORPAH and RUTH'S MOTHER speak English with a Moabite accent. NAOMI speaks with Bethlehem's (ROADMAP §5.7).

Naomi never says Ruth's name in this scene. She says it for the first time in Act Three (3:1).
*/

EXT. MOABITE VILLAGE — RUTH'S PARENTS' HOUSE — BEFORE DAWN

[[P6·20. Ruth's parents are alive: 2:11.]]

Grey light on the plateau. Wet grass. Somewhere a rooster starts, thinks better of it, and stops.

RUTH (26) stands in the doorway with a bundle on her back. Her forearms are bound in strips of dark cloth over cuts that haven't healed. [[P6·18]]

RUTH'S MOTHER (about 50) sets her lamp on the step and goes straight for the strap of the bundle.

RUTH'S MOTHER
You never could tie a knot.
[[D]]

She unties it and ties it again, harder.

Inside the house, RUTH'S FATHER sits by the cold hearth with his back to the door.

RUTH'S MOTHER
When will you be back?
[[D]]

Ruth doesn't answer.

Her mother's hands stop on the strap. Then they finish the knot anyway.

RUTH'S MOTHER
Their god took your husband. He took everything that woman had. And you'll follow her to him?
[[D]]

RUTH
They're my family.
[[D]]

Her mother looks at her for a long moment. Then she goes inside, past the father, who doesn't move, and comes back with something closed in her fist.

A little clay goddess, the length of a thumb, worn smooth from handling. [[P2: household figurines are common finds across the region]]

She presses it into Ruth's hand and folds Ruth's fingers over it.

RUTH'S MOTHER
Then take her. Somebody should look after you.
[[D]]

Ruth looks past her mother, into the house.

RUTH
Father.
[[D]]

He doesn't turn. But his hand, resting on his knee, closes into a fist and stays that way. [[P6: Moab layered, not all good or all bad]]

Down the lane, two shapes wait in the half-dark with bundles on their backs: NAOMI (48) and ORPAH (26). Orpah lifts a hand.

Ruth kisses her mother, tucks the goddess into her sash, and goes.

Her mother stands in the doorway with the lamp and watches the three of them past the last house, the way Boaz once watched Elimelech's family go. [[P6·20]]

EXT. PLATEAU ROAD — MORNING

[[P1: 1:7, "they went on the way to return to the land of Judah"]]

The plateau rolls west, green after the winter rains, with red poppies in the wild barley. Three women on the road, Naomi a few steps ahead.

Orpah walks backward in front of Ruth, talking. She's never been able to stand a silence.

ORPAH
Is it far?
[[D]]

NAOMI
Far enough.
[[D]]

ORPAH
Is there a well?
[[D]]

NAOMI
By the gate.
[[D. P1: 2 Sam 23:15, "the well of Bethlehem that is by the gate." The well David's men will fight their way to.]]

ORPAH
(to Ruth, delighted)
By the gate. We'll know everybody's business in a week.
[[D]]

Ruth smiles. But she's watching Naomi.

ORPAH
Will they like us?
[[D]]

Naomi doesn't answer.

Ruth hears what that means. Orpah doesn't, or decides not to, and turns around to walk forward again.

Naomi's lips are moving. No sound. She's saying something to herself, over and over, the way you rehearse a thing you don't want to say. [[P6·21]]

Ruth sees it.

EXT. EDGE OF THE PLATEAU — CONTINUOUS

The road tops a last rise, and the world falls away.

Below them is the rift: a thousand meters of broken slope, then the Jordan, a green thread through brown country. To the south lies the Salt Sea, flat and pale. And across all of it, blue in the haze, the hills of Judah. [[P2: dossier §6. P6·21: the second of three views across the rift]]

Naomi stops. The other two stop behind her.

For a while she just looks. Somewhere over there is Bethlehem.

She sets down her bundle and turns to them. She opens her mouth, and nothing comes.

Her face does it first. The arithmetic: three mouths, no field, no man, and a town that won't want two Moabite widows. Then the pain of it. Then a tiredness that goes all the way down. Then, slowly, something that settles. [[Your note: calculation, pain, exhaustion, acceptance]]

NAOMI
Go back.
[[B: 1:8]]

ORPAH
(a small, confused laugh)
What?
[[D]]

NAOMI
Both of you. Go home to your mothers.
[[B: 1:8. "Her mother's house": Ruth's is the house we just left.]]

Her voice cracks. She pushes through it.

NAOMI (CONT'D)
May God be as good to you as you've been to the dead. And to me.
[[B: 1:8]]

NAOMI (CONT'D)
May he give each of you the rest you deserve.
[[B: 1:9, your wording. The text adds "each of you in the house of her husband." To restore it: "...the rest you deserve. A husband. A home." See TREATMENT §7.]]

She kisses Orpah. She kisses Ruth. [[P1: 1:9]]

That's what breaks them. Orpah cries out loud, and then Ruth does. [[P1: 1:9, "they lifted up their voices and wept"]]

ORPAH
No, no. We're coming with you.

RUTH ^
We're coming with you.
[[B: 1:10. Dual dialogue: Orpah first, Ruth over her, not in unison.]]

NAOMI
Go back, my daughters. Why would you come with me?
[[B: 1:11]]

A laugh that isn't one. Her hand goes flat against her own stomach.

NAOMI (CONT'D)
Am I going to have more sons for you to marry?
[[B: 1:11, "sons in my womb." The gesture carries the image.]]

Nobody laughs.

NAOMI (CONT'D)
Go home. I'm too old for a husband. And even if I said there was hope, even if I had a husband tonight, and had sons... would you wait for them to grow up? Stay alone all those years?
[[B: 1:12–13]]

She shakes her head.

NAOMI (CONT'D)
No. This is far more bitter for me than for you.
[[B: 1:13. The Hebrew can also be read "very bitter for your sake."]]

NAOMI (CONT'D)
(quieter)
The Lord's hand has turned against me.
[[B: 1:13]]

They cry again, all three of them. [[P1: 1:14]]

Orpah wipes her face with both hands. She looks at Naomi for a long time. Then she steps in and kisses her. Not a protest this time. A goodbye. [[P1: 1:14]]

ORPAH
(into Naomi's shoulder)
Mother.
[[D]]

Naomi holds her tight, then lets her go.

Ruth hasn't moved. Her hand has closed on Naomi's sleeve, and it stays closed. [[P1: 1:14, "Ruth clung to her." The verb is the one in Gen 2:24.]]

Orpah turns to her.

ORPAH
Come with me.
[[D]]

Ruth shakes her head.

With her free hand she takes the clay goddess from her sash and presses it into Orpah's palm.

RUTH
Give her back to my mother.
[[D. P6·22]]

Orpah looks down at it, and understands. She takes Ruth's face in both hands and rests her forehead against Ruth's. Then she picks up her bundle and goes.

Down the road, alone, with the goddess in her fist. A little way off she turns and looks back. [[P6·22. She did what Naomi asked. The film never blames her.]]

Naomi watches her go. Then she looks down at Ruth's hand on her sleeve. She puts her own hand over it, and gently, one finger at a time, starts to work it loose.

NAOMI
Orpah has gone back to her people and her gods.
[[B: 1:15, your wording]]

NAOMI (CONT'D)
Go after her.
[[B: 1:15]]

Ruth looks back down the road. Orpah, getting smaller. Past her, the smudge of smoke over the village. Her mother's house is in there somewhere.

Then the wind comes.

It comes up out of the desert behind them, out of the east, hot and dry. It flattens the grass toward the edge. It pulls Ruth's shawl forward and leans on her back like a hand, toward the land across the rift. [[P5·23. Deniable: she can read it as a sign, and the audience can read it as weather. Hot east winds are typical of spring.]]

Ruth doesn't look up at the sky. For a moment she doesn't look at anything. Something in her face goes still.

She turns back to Naomi. Her hand is still on Naomi's sleeve. Naomi never got it loose.

RUTH
Don't ask me to leave you, or to turn back.
[[B: 1:16, your wording]]

Naomi starts to answer...

RUTH (CONT'D)
Where you go, I'll go. Where you stay, I'll stay. Your people will be my people, and your God my God.
[[B: 1:16]]

RUTH (CONT'D)
Where you die, I'll die, and that's where I'll be buried.
[[B: 1:17]]

A beat. Then, steady, every word meant:

RUTH (CONT'D)
May Yahweh do that to me, and worse, if anything but death comes between us.
[[B: 1:17. The first time she says the Name, and she says it in an oath (ROADMAP §5.5).]]

Naomi looks at her for a long time. We watch her take it in: the girl who brought bread to a grave nobody else came to, the girl at her oven for ten years, the girl who won't let go.

Then Naomi nods. Once. Small and direct, and accepting. [[P6·24, your note]]

She says nothing more. [[P1: 1:18]]

She bends for her bundle. Ruth gets there first, swings it up onto her shoulder beside her own, and Naomi lets her.

They start down.

EXT. EDGE OF THE PLATEAU — CONTINUOUS — WIDE

Two small figures going down into the rift.

Far behind them on the road east, a third figure has stopped to watch them go. After a while she turns and walks home.

CUT TO:

[[Act One continues with the river crossing at Jericho (P4·25).]]

